  "headline_index": 0,
  "what_the_study_did": "The study proposes incorporating a 'robustness' test into preclinical research to improve the translation of findings from animal models to human patients.",
  "what_it_found": "Preclinical results frequently fail to translate to clinical settings, with only 11% of oncology findings being validated in clinical trials, and common animal models are inconsistent predictors of human toxic responses.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Only 11% of published “landmark” findings in oncology were validated in clinical trials ( 2 ), consistent with other disease areas, due to an inability to demonstrate safety and efficacy ( Figure 1 ).",
      "topic": "Low Translation Rate"
    },
    {
      "quote": "An analysis of over 2,000 drugs found that common genetically homogeneous preclinical species are inconsistent predictors of toxic responses in humans ( 3 ).",
      "topic": "Inconsistent Predictors"
    },
    {
      "quote": "However, retrospective studies using heterogeneous models support their utility for predicting therapeutic safety.",
      "topic": "Heterogeneous Models"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Genetic background profoundly affects the robustness of EGFR inhibition in preclinical CRC models, as most colon tumors are EGFR dependent in B6J mice while being EGFR independent in humans ( 23 ), providing evidence for the observed lack of translation.",
      "topic": "Genetic Context Impact"
    },
    {
      "quote": "These studies suggested that the outcomes of ERBB3 clinical trials would have been predicted using a robustness assay, which could have led to a more informed clinical trial.",
      "topic": "Predictive Power of Robustness"
    },
    {
      "quote": "By expanding the National Institutes of Health’s directive on rigor and reproducibility to the 3Rs of rigor, reproducibility, and robustness that embraces genetic heterogeneity as an inherent biological phenomenon, translational studies will have a greater impact by improving clinical study design, ultimately leading to improved health and reduction of disease.",
      "topic": "Embracing Heterogeneity"
    }
  ]
